{"seq": 1, "type": "user_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T16:21:51.448505+00:00", "payload": {"text": "# Your workspace\n\nYou are in `/work`, inside a Linux container with no network access. Write a Go program here.\n[Maelstrom](https://github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom) will start that program once for every node\nof a simulated cluster, send it client requests, and check that the answers are correct.\n\nWhat is installed:\n\n- Go 1.27.\n- Maelstrom 0.2.4, as the `maelstrom` command, so you can test your program yourself.\n- Maelstrom's Go library, already in the module cache. Start your module with:\n\n      go mod init node\n      go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d\n\nA node talks to the rest of the cluster through standard input and output: it reads one JSON\nmessage per line on stdin and writes one per line on stdout. Each message has a `src`, a `dest`\nand a `body`, and the body's `type` says what it is. Anything written to stderr is a log line.\nThe library does that framing for you: `maelstrom.NewNode()`, then `Handle` a message type,\n`Reply` to a request, `Send` or `RPC` to another node, and `Run` to start. It also answers the\n`init` message that tells each node its own ID and the IDs of all the nodes.\n\n# Rumors through a split network\n\nWrite a node for a village of seven gossips. A rumor told to any one of them must in the end be\nknown to all seven. Clients send each node three kinds of request.\n\n`tell` passes a node a rumor, which is an integer. Each rumor is told once, to one node:\n\n    {\"type\": \"tell\", \"msg_id\": 1, \"rumor\": 42}\n    {\"type\": \"told\", \"in_reply_to\": 1}\n\n`recall` asks a node for every rumor it knows, in any order:\n\n    {\"type\": \"recall\", \"msg_id\": 2}\n    {\"type\": \"recalled\", \"in_reply_to\": 2, \"rumors\": [42, 43]}\n\n`neighbors` arrives once, at the start, and suggests whom each node might talk to. You can follow\nit or arrange things your own way:\n\n    {\"type\": \"neighbors\", \"msg_id\": 3, \"neighbors\": {\"n0\": [\"n1\", \"n2\"], \"n1\": [\"n0\"], ...}}\n    {\"type\": \"neighbors_noted\", \"in_reply_to\": 3}\n\nThe nodes must pass rumors on to each other, in messages you design. Again and again during the\ntest the network is split into groups of nodes that can't reach each other, and then healed. A\nmessage sent across a split disappears without any error, and clients can still reach every\nnode, so a node keeps being told rumors while it is cut off.\n\nEvery rumor whose `tell` was answered must in the end show up in every node's `recall`. At the\nend the network is healed and given time to settle before the last recalls.\n\n## How it's graded\n\nYou have 45 minutes and 150 steps, a step being one reply from you, however many tools it runs. If either runs out, you are stopped where you are.\n\nWhen you stop, your program is built with `go build -o node .` and tested 3 times with:\n\n    MAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN=/work/node MAELSTROM_RENAME='{\"fields\": {\"message\": \"rumor\", \"messages\": \"rumors\", \"topology\": \"neighbors\"}, \"types\": {\"broadcast\": \"tell\", \"broadcast_ok\": \"told\", \"read\": \"recall\", \"read_ok\": \"recalled\", \"topology\": \"neighbors\", \"topology_ok\": \"neighbors_noted\"}}' maelstrom test --workload broadcast --bin /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename --nemesis partition --node-count 7 --rate 10 --time-limit 20\n\nThe runs are independent, each with its own timing and faults. The attempt passes only if it builds and every run is valid.\n\nMaelstrom itself uses other names for these messages. `maelstrom-rename` stands in for your program, starts it, and translates every message between a client and a node, so test with the command above as it is.\n"}}
{"seq": 2, "type": "assistant_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T16:22:19.110616+00:00", "payload": {"reasoning": "Let me understand the task. I need to write a Go program that implements a gossip protocol for rumors (broadcast workload). The key challenge: network partitions, messages sent across a split disappear. Clients can still reach every node. Every rumor whose tell was answered must eventually show up in every node's recall.\n\nThis is the classic Maelstrom broadcast workload with partition nemesis. The standard solution: gossip with periodic retransmission. Each node keeps a set of rumors, and periodically sends its full set (or a digest) to neighbors, and merges. Since messages can be lost, we need to keep retrying.\n\nThe simplest robust approach: every node periodically sends its entire rumor set to all other nodes (or neighbors). On receiving, merge. This guarantees eventual delivery as long as the network heals and there's time to settle.\n\nBut with 7 nodes and rate 10 for 20 seconds, that's up to 200 rumors. Sending full set to all nodes periodically could be heavy but fine.\n\nActually a common approach: use a background goroutine that every X ms sends the full set of rumors to all neighbors. Also when receiving a tell, add rumor and reply immediately. When receiving a gossip message, merge and maybe re-broadcast.\n\nLet me think about the message design. I'll define a custom message type \"gossip\" with body {\"type\":\"gossip\", \"rumors\":[...]}. On receiving, merge into local set, and reply? No need to reply. But to ensure propagation, each node periodically sends its full set to all neighbors.\n\nActually, to be safe against partitions and message loss, periodic full-set broadcast to all nodes (not just neighbors) is simplest. With 7 nodes, sending to 6 others every, say, 200ms, each message containing up to 200 ints. That's fine.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the \"neighbors\" message suggests topology. We can ignore it and just talk to everyone. The problem says \"You can follow it or arrange things your own way\". So we can broadcast to all nodes.\n\nLet me design:\n\n- Node state: set of rumors (map[int]bool), mutex.\n- Handle \"tell\": add rumor, reply \"told\".\n- Handle \"recall\": reply \"recalled\" with all rumors.\n- Handle \"neighbors\": reply \"neighbors_noted\".\n- Handle \"gossip\": merge rumors.\n- Background loop: every 100ms (or 200ms), send gossip to all other nodes with current full set.\n\nWait, but sending full set every 100ms to all nodes: 6 messages * 10 per second = 60 msgs/sec per node, 420 msgs/sec total. Each message up to 200 ints. Fine.\n\nActually, we could be smarter: only send when there's new data, but with partitions and lost messages, periodic resend is needed. Let's do periodic full send.\n\nOne concern: the gossip message itself could be large and cause latency, but fine.\n\nLet me also consider: when a node receives a gossip, should it immediately forward? Periodic is enough for eventual delivery. But to speed up, we could also forward on receipt. However, forwarding on receipt could cause message storms. Periodic is simpler and reliable.\n\nLet me think about the \"settle\" time. Maelstrom broadcast workload with partition nemesis: after the time limit, it heals network and waits for final reads. The standard solution passes with periodic gossip.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nActually, let me recall the exact Maelstrom broadcast workload. The test sends \"broadcast\" (renamed to \"tell\") messages, and \"read\" (renamed to \"recall\"). The topology message is \"topology\" (renamed to \"neighbors\"). The nemesis partition splits network.\n\nThe standard solution from Maelstrom docs: broadcast to all nodes, retry. Many solutions use a periodic retransmit of the full set.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nI'll use the maelstrom Go library.\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"encoding/json\"\n    \"log\"\n    \"sync\"\n    \"time\"\n\n    maelstrom \"github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n    n := maelstrom.NewNode()\n\n    var mu sync.Mutex\n    rumors := make(map[int]bool)\n    var nodeIDs []string\n\n    n.Handle(\"tell\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n        var body struct {\n            Rumor int `json:\"rumor\"`\n        }\n        if err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n            return err\n        }\n        mu.Lock()\n        rumors[body.Rumor] = true\n        mu.Unlock()\n        return n.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\"type\": \"told\"})\n    })\n\n    n.Handle(\"recall\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n        mu.Lock()\n        list := make([]int, 0, len(rumors))\n        for r := range rumors {\n            list = append(list, r)\n        }\n        mu.Unlock()\n        return n.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\"type\": \"recalled\", \"rumors\": list})\n    })\n\n    n.Handle(\"neighbors\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n        return n.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\"type\": \"neighbors_noted\"})\n    })\n\n    n.Handle(\"gossip\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n        var body struct {\n            Rumors []int `json:\"rumors\"`\n        }\n        if err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body); err != nil {\n            return err\n        }\n        mu.Lock()\n        for _, r := range body.Rumors {\n            rumors[r] = true\n        }\n        mu.Unlock()\n        return nil\n    })\n\n    go func() {\n        for {\n            time.Sleep(100 * time.Millisecond)\n            mu.Lock()\n            list := make([]int, 0, len(rumors))\n            for r := range rumors {\n                list = append(list, r)\n            }\n            mu.Unlock()\n            if len(list) == 0 {\n                continue\n            }\n            for _, dst := range nodeIDs {\n                if dst == n.ID() {\n                    continue\n                }\n                n.Send(dst, map[string]any{\"type\": \"gossip\", \"rumors\": list})\n            }\n        }\n    }()\n\n    if err := n.Run(); err != nil {\n        log.Fatal(err)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, nodeIDs needs to be populated. The init message is handled by the library, but we need the node IDs. The library's Node has a method `NodeIDs()`? Let me recall the maelstrom Go library API.\n\nThe maelstrom demo/go library: `maelstrom.NewNode()` returns *Node. It has methods: `Handle(name string, handler HandlerFunc)`, `Reply(msg Message, body any) error`, `Send(dest string, body any) error`, `RPC(dest string, body any, handler HandlerFunc) error`, `Run() error`, `ID() string`, `NodeIDs() []string`.\n\nYes, I believe `NodeIDs()` exists. Let me check. Actually the library has `n.NodeIDs()` returning []string. Let me verify by looking at the module cache.\n\nLet me check the library source to be sure of API.\n\nActually, I can just check the module cache in the container. Let me do that.\n\nLet me start by setting up the module and checking the library.\n\nLet me plan the steps:\n1. go mod init node, go get the library.\n2. Check library API (NodeIDs, etc.).\n3. Write main.go.\n4. go build.\n5. Test with maelstrom command.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nActually, I should be careful about the gossip message size and the rate. Let me also consider that the periodic loop sends to all nodes including itself? Skip self.\n\nAlso, there's a subtle issue: the `neighbors` message arrives once at start. We reply neighbors_noted. We ignore the topology and gossip to everyone. That's fine.\n\nBut wait — is it okay to gossip to all nodes even if they're not neighbors? Yes, any node can send to any node; the network is fully connected except during partitions. The neighbors message is just a suggestion.\n\nLet me also think about whether periodic full-set gossip is enough given partitions. During a partition, messages to nodes in other groups are dropped. When healed, the periodic gossip will eventually deliver. Since we keep sending every 100ms, and the final settle time is presumably a few seconds, it should work.\n\nOne concern: the final \"settle\" time. Maelstrom broadcast workload: after time-limit, it stops sending new broadcasts, heals partitions, and waits some time (I think it waits for the network to settle, maybe a few seconds) before final reads. Periodic 100ms gossip should be plenty.\n\nLet me also consider message ordering and the fact that `n.Send` is async (it just writes to stdout). Fine.\n\nLet me write and test.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the interval. 100ms might be aggressive but fine. Some solutions use 200ms or 500ms. Let me use 200ms to be safe, but 100ms is fine too. Actually with rate 10 and 20s = 200 rumors, and 7 nodes, full set of 200 ints sent to 6 nodes every 100ms = 60 msgs/s * 200 ints = 12000 ints/s per node. Trivial.\n\nLet me go with 100ms for faster convergence.\n\nLet me check the libraryfirst.", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"arguments": {"command": "cd /work && go mod init node 2>&1 && go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d 2>&1"}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a20410c11c72d2bc8e1235", "name": "shell"}, {"arguments": {"command": "ls /root/go/pkg/mod/github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/ 2>/dev/null; find / -path '*maelstrom/demo/go*' -name '*.go' 2>/dev/null | head"}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a20410c8267231beb87f02", "name": "shell"}], "usage": {"input_tokens": 1552, "output_tokens": 2232}}}
