mors))\n        for r := range rumors {\n            list = append(list, r)\n        }\n        mu.Unlock()\n        if len(list) == 0 {\n            continue\n        }\n        for _, id := range n.NodeIDs() {\n            if id != n.ID() {\n                n.Send(id, map[string]any{\"type\": \"gossip\", \"rumors\": list})\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}()\n```\n\nThis calls NodeIDs() after the first 100ms sleep, by which time init is processed. This is robust.\n\nActually, even more robust: I could wait for init. But the sleep-first approach is fine because init is the very first message and Run processes it immediately.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtlety: what if the gossip loop's first iteration happens before init? The sleep is 100ms, and init is processed within microseconds of Run starting. So after 100ms, init is definitely done. Good.\n\nLet me also consider: should I send gossip even when list is empty? No need.\n\nLet me also think about whether I should reduce the interval or add immediate forwarding. With 10s settle time and 100ms interval, it's fine.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is there any issue with the gossip message being dropped due to the rename? The rename translates \"message\"->\"rumor\", \"messages\"->\"rumors\", \"topology\"->\"neighbors\", and types broadcast->tell etc. My custom \"gossip\" type is not in the rename map, so it passes through unchanged. The \"rumors\" field in my gossip message — wait, the rename maps \"messages\" -> \"rumors\". My gossip message uses field \"rumors\". Does the rename affect my \"rumors\" field?\n\nLet me think about how maelstrom-rename works. It translates messages between client and node. The rename config maps field names: \"message\" -> \"rumor\", \"messages\" -> \"rumors\", \"topology\" -> \"neighbors\". And type names: \"broadcast\" -> \"tell\", etc.\n\nThe rename is applied to messages between the client (Maelstrom test) and the node. But my node-to-node gossip messages don't go through the client; they go directly between nodes. So the rename doesn't affect them.\n\nWait, actually, how does maelstrom-rename work? It's a wrapper around the node binary. It intercepts stdin/stdout of the node and translates. But node-to-node messages are sent via the node's stdout to Maelstrom's network, which routes them to other nodes' stdin. The rename wrapper is per-node, so it would translate ALL messages going in/out of that node, including node-to-node gossip messages.\n\nHmm, this is important. Let me understand maelstrom-rename.\n\nActually, let me look at how maelstrom-rename works. It's at /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename. Let me check.\n\nActually, the rename config maps \"messages\" -> \"rumors\". If my gossip message has a field \"rumors\", and the rename maps \"messages\" -> \"rumors\", then... the rename would translate a field named \"messages\" to \"rumors\". But my field is already \"rumors\". Would it translate \"rumors\" to something? No, the rename is directional: it renames \"messages\" to \"rumors\" (and presumably \"rumors\" back to \"messages\" in the other direction?).\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me understand the rename mechanism precisely.\n\nActually, the key insight: the rename is bidirectional. The Maelstrom test uses \"broadcast\"/\"read\"/\"topology\" and fields \"message\"/\"messages\"/\"topology\". The node (my program) uses \"tell\"/\"recall\"/\"neighbors\" and fields \"rumor\"/\"rumors\"/\"neighbors\". The rename wrapper translates between these two vocabularies.\n\nFor node-to-node messages, the rename wrapper would also translate them. But my gossip messages use type \"gossip\" and field \"rumors\". The rename config doesn't mention \"gossip\", so the type passes through. But the field \"rumors\" — the rename maps \"messages\" -> \"rumors\". \n\nLet me look at the actual maelstrom-rename source to understand.\n\nActually, let me check if there's a source for maelstrom-rename.", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"arguments": {"command": "w